  <!-- ============ WHY FIFTEEN ============ -->
  <section class="why15">
    <div class="why15-in">
      <div class="why15-k">A note on the cast</div>
      <div class="why15-grid">
        <p class="why15-lead">The model reads all <b>196 countries</b>. The next four pages follow <b>15</b>,
        because a chart with 196 lines on it is a texture, not an argument.</p>
        <div class="why15-body">
          <p>They were picked to make the comparison hard to dismiss, on four tests:</p>
          <ol class="why15-list">
            <li><b>Spread.</b> From Guatemala at 163.3 cm to the Netherlands at 183.5 — a 20.2 cm range that covers
            <b>84%</b> of the world's spread for this cohort, with 97% of all countries falling inside it.</li>
            <li><b>Natural experiments.</b> Pairs close enough in ancestry that genes cannot explain the difference:
            the two Koreas, Netherlands and Germany, Sweden and Denmark.</li>
            <li><b>Money against outcome.</b> India at $3,175 a head through to Denmark at $57,266, so wealth can be
            tested against height rather than assumed to equal it.</li>
            <li><b>Awkward cases.</b> The ones that break a tidy story get a seat too — the richest country that
            stopped growing, the poor country that grew anyway, the famine cohorts.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="why15-cav">This is an <b>editorial</b> selection, not a statistical sample, and it is the one
          judgement call in the deck. Nothing in the model depends on it: the 196-country result stands whichever
          fifteen you put on stage. Guatemala and Nigeria were chosen for similar poverty and unrelated gene pools;
          North Korea is included despite thin data, and its file says so.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

