  <details class="readme" id="faq">
    <summary>FAQ <b>· the questions a sceptic asks first</b></summary>
    <div class="readme-body">

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Is R² = 0.95 correct?</h5>
        <p>R² = 0.95 on national-level population averages (not individuals). These are smooth
        statistics — high R² is expected when the signal is real.</p>
        <p>The precise number is <b>0.959</b> (male 0.960, female 0.958) on held-out folds. That
        tells you 11 childhood-environment indicators account for almost all the variation in mean
        height <em>between countries</em>. It does not mean the model can predict any individual
        person's height — within a country, genetics and individual circumstance dominate.</p>
        <p>Two harder tests keep the headline honest:</p>
        <table class="rm-tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Male R²</th><th>Female R²</th><th>MAE</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Held-out folds</td><td>0.960</td><td>0.958</td><td>0.64 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Temporal — train ≤1988, predict after</td><td>0.830</td><td>0.806</td><td>1.39 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Leave-country-out — a country never seen</td><td>0.591</td><td>0.563</td><td>2.32 cm</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>So: 0.96 describes how well these indicators track variation <em>in this dataset</em>.
        <b>0.59</b> is what you should expect if you hand the model a country it has never met. Both
        are correct — they answer different questions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Doesn't R² that high just mean the model is overfitting?</h5>
        <p>Partly, and the drop from 0.96 to 0.59 under leave-country-out is exactly that effect made
        visible. Three things argue the signal is nonetheless real: the male and female models are
        trained <b>separately</b> and independently rank the same top five drivers; the temporal split
        still reaches <b>0.83</b> predicting cohorts born after 1988 from cohorts born before; and no
        country identifier is available to the model at any point.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Does the model know which country it is looking at?</h5>
        <p>No. Country identity, country code and region are all excluded from the feature set, along
        with sex — hence two separate models. The 11 inputs are all childhood-environment measurements.
        It cannot memorise "this row is the Netherlands"; it has to reach 183 cm from physician density,
        protein supply and the rest.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Isn't height mostly genetic?</h5>
        <p>Within a population, yes — roughly <b>80%</b> heritable. Between populations, no. Genes
        determine who is tall relative to their neighbours; they do not explain why the average moved
        <b>+14.8 cm</b> in South Korea across a century while it moved <b>+4.2 cm</b> in India. Gene
        pools do not change measurably in three generations. Food, sanitation and clinics do.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Correlation or causation?</h5>
        <p><b>Correlation, at the country level.</b> Every association here is ecological: it describes
        countries, not individuals, and it cannot rule out confounding. What raises it above pure
        correlation is direction and mechanism — protein and infection have known biological pathways to
        growth, the effect appears with the expected generational lag, and the ranking survives being
        re-derived separately for each sex. Read it as strong, consistent evidence, not proof.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Why does GDP rank only 7th? That seems wrong.</h5>
        <p>It is a model output, not an opinion: GDP per capita carries <b>4.5%</b> average importance
        against physician density's <b>37.2%</b>. The reading is not that money is irrelevant — rich
        countries do tend to be taller — but that money is a <em>proxy</em>, and once you also give the
        model the things money buys (doctors, protein, sanitation, equality), GDP has little left to
        explain. India and China are the cleanest illustration: near-identical GDP per head, and an
        <b>8.8 cm</b> gap.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Why is North Korea's line different from the atlas?</h5>
        <p>Because two sources disagree and we would rather show that than hide it. NCD-RisC puts the
        1996 cohort at 173.7 cm, but with a <b>95% interval of 162.0–186.3 cm</b> — a band 24 cm wide,
        the widest of all 200 countries, because there is effectively no survey data for the DPRK. The
        Korea mirror therefore uses refugee anthropometry (Pak, Schwekendiek &amp; Kim 2011, 6,512
        refugees born 1930s–1980s), which is the empirical basis for the widely quoted gap and which
        carries its own selection bias. The atlas and door frame stay on NCD-RisC so that all 164
        shaded countries are measured the same way.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Is the 1900 end of the door frame real data?</h5>
        <p>No, and it is labelled as such throughout. This dataset begins at the <b>1966</b> birth
        cohort. Only the <b>1970, 1980, 1990 and 1996</b> points on each bar are measured; 1900–1960 is
        interpolated from a 1900 anchor to the measured 1970 value. The summary panel beside the door
        frame flips to "Reconstructed cohort" whenever you scrub before 1970.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Why 15 countries when the model uses 196?</h5>
        <p>The model reads all 196. The narrative follows 15 because a 196-line chart is a texture
        rather than an argument. The fifteen span 84% of the world's height range for this cohort,
        include the ancestry-matched pairs, and stretch from $3,175 to $57,266 per head. It is an
        editorial selection and the deck says so — no model result depends on it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-q">
        <h5>Taller is better, then?</h5>
        <p>No, and nothing here argues it. Above roughly 175 cm the health returns flatten and some
        risks rise. The claim is narrower: a population's <em>change</em> in average height is an
        unusually honest record of how well its children were fed, healed and protected. Height is the
        receipt, not the prize.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </details>
